The non-commutative analogues extend results from commutative settings in braided monoidal categories by generalizing the notion of smoothness (or "quasifreeness") of Hopf algebras within a semisimple abelian braided monoidal category C that satisfies certain conditions. Specifically, the equivalences that characterize smoothness in the commutative case extend to the non-commutative context as follows:

- (α) H is smooth;
- (β) H is H-smooth as a right H-comodule algebra;
- (γ) H is Q-smooth as a right Q-comodule algebra for every quotient Hopf algebra Q of H such that H is coflat as a left Q-comodule;
- (δ) H is left hereditary as an algebra in C, meaning any H-submodule of a projective left H-module is projective.

A key point in the proof is the equivalence between (β) and the vanishing of the second Hochschild cohomology H²(H, M) in C for every left H-module M, where M is regarded as an (H, H)-bimodule with trivial right H-action via the counit. This cohomology is naturally isomorphic to the Ext² group in C. This shows that the concept of smoothness can be extended to non-commutative Hopf algebras in braided monoidal categories by considering equivariant smoothness and homological conditions analogous to those in the commutative case [1].
